1
00:00:00,578 --> 00:00:02,761
You spent an hour on your title and your
thumbnail.

2
00:00:03,323 --> 00:00:06,181
But there's one box almost every creator
leaves empty.

3
00:00:06,840 --> 00:00:09,312
And it's the one that tells YouTube what
you actually said.

4
00:00:10,565 --> 00:00:11,565
It's subtitles.

5
00:00:11,914 --> 00:00:16,375
Not the auto-captions YouTube makes for
you, but your own subtitle file, uploaded

6
00:00:16,375 --> 00:00:17,375
with the video.

7
00:00:17,854 --> 00:00:21,066
Here's why it matters, and how to add one
in about 5 minutes.

8
00:00:22,334 --> 00:00:26,951
YouTube's own help page says search looks
at how well your title, tags, description,

9
00:00:26,951 --> 00:00:29,510
and video content match what people search
for.

10
00:00:30,858 --> 00:00:32,430
Your title is a few words.

11
00:00:32,944 --> 00:00:34,758
Your description, a few lines.

12
00:00:35,352 --> 00:00:39,091
But a 10-minute video can hold around
1,500 spoken words.

13
00:00:40,472 --> 00:00:44,388
And YouTube describes a subtitle file as
the text of what is said in the video.

14
00:00:44,966 --> 00:00:49,973
Every word, every topic, every keyword you
mention, in a form YouTube can read.

15
00:00:51,225 --> 00:00:53,520
But YouTube already makes auto-captions,
right?

16
00:00:54,114 --> 00:00:55,397
It does, but they're a guess.

17
00:00:55,990 --> 00:00:59,436
Names, brand words, and niche terms are
where they slip most.

18
00:00:59,965 --> 00:01:01,408
And those are often your keywords.

19
00:01:02,771 --> 00:01:03,771
There's a second win.

20
00:01:04,132 --> 00:01:07,243
Many people watch with the sound off or in
their second language.

21
00:01:07,820 --> 00:01:11,363
YouTube says captions help you share your
videos with a larger audience.

22
00:01:11,908 --> 00:01:13,976
And viewers who can follow along keep
watching.

23
00:01:14,633 --> 00:01:16,781
Watch time is one of the signals YouTube
uses.

24
00:01:18,176 --> 00:01:22,699
To be fair, subtitles won't rescue a weak
video, and YouTube doesn't say how much

25
00:01:22,699 --> 00:01:23,699
they count.

26
00:01:23,850 --> 00:01:26,479
They're simply the cheapest SEO win most
channels skip.

27
00:01:27,876 --> 00:01:28,876
Here's the workflow.

28
00:01:29,305 --> 00:01:34,451
Open TranscriptX, click Upload, and pick
your video from your computer, Google

29
00:01:34,451 --> 00:01:36,322
Drive, Dropbox, or OneDrive.

30
00:01:37,590 --> 00:01:42,343
TranscriptX turns your speech into text
with a timestamp on every paragraph.

31
00:01:43,596 --> 00:01:47,660
Give it a quick read and fix any names
with the Edit button so your keywords are

32
00:01:47,660 --> 00:01:48,660
spelled right.

33
00:01:49,713 --> 00:01:51,736
Then click Download and pick SRT.

34
00:01:53,499 --> 00:01:55,582
Now open your video in YouTube Studio.

35
00:01:56,127 --> 00:01:57,681
Your title and description are set.

36
00:01:58,290 --> 00:01:59,540
Next, click Subtitles.

37
00:02:00,998 --> 00:02:05,820
Add your language, choose Upload File,
then With Timing, and select your SRT

38
00:02:05,820 --> 00:02:06,820
file.

39
00:02:07,406 --> 00:02:10,114
Your subtitles line up with the video,
line by line.

40
00:02:10,770 --> 00:02:11,909
Click Done and Publish.

41
00:02:13,286 --> 00:02:16,282
Title, thumbnail, tags, and now subtitles.

42
00:02:16,795 --> 00:02:18,013
Try it on your next upload.

43
00:02:18,477 --> 00:02:20,993
Get your SRT free at transcripts.ai.
